======================================
FreeDCE RPC and DCOM Toolkit for Linux
======================================

This package provides support for developing DCE RPC and Microsoft RPC
applications on the Linux platform. The source code and binaries are
freely-redistributable provided you maintains the copyright notice of the
source components.

This package could be ported to other platforms as well, provided that you can
implement the semantics of the DCE Exceptions and DCE Threads on the target
platforms' thread layer.

FreeDCE for Linux relies on GNU Libc 2.x and LinuxThreads Pthreads.  DCE
Threads and DCE Exceptions are emulated using LinuxThreads, allowing for
multithreaded RPC client and server applications to coexist with other
threaded and non-threaded library components in the LInux development
environment.

Inventory of Goods:
-------------------
* OSF DCE 1.1 IDL compiler
* DCE RPC runtime and development libraries/headers
* `uuid' tool for generating and manipulating DCE UUIDs
* rpcd - the host endpoint mapper for DCE RPC applications
* Performance, Interoperabilit and Functionality test suite
* some demonstration code for writing clients and servers

Prerequisites:
-------------

A C compiler with C23 support (the IDL compiler uses it; gcc 13 or
later), a C++ compiler (for the uuid tool), make, autoconf, automake,
libtool, flex and bison.  Tested on x86_64 with:

 Ubuntu 26.04:  gcc 15.2, autoconf 2.72, automake 1.18, libtool 2.5.4
 AlmaLinux 10:  gcc 14.3, autoconf 2.71, automake 1.16.5, libtool 2.4.7

and flex 2.6.4, bison 3.8.2 on both.  The packages:

 Debian, Ubuntu:
  # apt install build-essential autoconf automake libtool flex bison
 RHEL 10 and compatible (AlmaLinux 10):
  # dnf install gcc gcc-c++ make autoconf automake libtool flex bison \
      diffutils file

How to Build:
-------------

FreeDCE needs the dcethreads library (directory dcethreads of the
repository); build and install it first, with the same prefix.  In a git
checkout, buildconf creates the configure scripts.  From the top
directory of the repository:

 % cd dcethreads
 % ./buildconf
 % ./configure --prefix=/opt/dce
 % make
 % make install
 % cd ../freedce
 % ./buildconf
 % ./configure --prefix=/opt/dce
 % make
 % make install

/opt/dce is the default prefix; pass the same --prefix to both configure
calls to change it.  "make install" needs write access to the prefix.
Building out of tree (configure called from an empty directory) works as
well.  With another autoconf version, buildconf rewrites the comments of
the tracked templates dcethreads_conf.h.in and dce_config.h.in.

Tests: "make check" in dcethreads, in freedce/perf (one test needs a
running rpcd) and in freedce/tests/loadtest (a short load test).

The installed programs find the libraries through their run path; other
programs need LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/dce/lib or an entry in
/etc/ld.so.conf.d.  The endpoint mapper rpcd listens on port 135 and has
to run as root:

 # /opt/dce/bin/rpcd

(-f keeps it in the foreground.)  The install does not start rpcd at boot;
freedce/rpcd contains the SysV init scripts rc.dcerpcd and rc.dce-clean.

How to build your own programs that use DCE RPC
-----------------------------------------------

CFLAGS+=-D_REENTRANT -D_GNU_SOURCE
LDFLAGS+= -ldcerpc -ldcethreads -lpthread
The UUID functions (uuid_create etc.) are part of libdcerpc; do not link
-luuid (that is the unrelated util-linux library). A program that uses the
util-linux libuuid itself must call it from a separate shared library: both
libraries define uuid_compare, with different arguments.

Runtime settings (ncacn_ip_tcp)
-------------------------------

RPC_CN_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT: the idle time in seconds after which a server
asks a client to close its connection (default 120, 0 = never).  A
connection that the client neither closes nor uses in the 30 seconds after
that is closed by the server.  The last connection of a client that holds
context handles is kept.  When accept runs out of descriptors, the server
reclaims connections idle for 5 seconds at once (shutdown request, closed
after 5 seconds).

Bind timeout: a client gives up waiting for the answer to its bind after
6 times the com timeout of the binding (rpc_mgmt_set_com_timeout; 30
seconds by default, at least 5 seconds, no limit with
rpc_c_binding_infinite_timeout) with rpc_s_connect_timed_out.

TCP keepalive: servers and clients set SO_KEEPALIVE on their connections;
the kernel settings net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time/intvl/probes decide how
fast a vanished peer is detected (default: after about 2 hours).

